es the health issues that affect the aging population, contributing to a decreased quality of life and various health complications. Overall, morbidity is a critical factor in health management, highlighting the importance of addressing these issues to improve patient outcomes.\",7\r\n2217,HEMOCROMATOSIS,\"Hemochromatosis is a medical condition characterized by excessive iron absorption and accumulation in the body, which can lead to significant health issues. This iron overload primarily affects various organs, including the liver, and can result in severe complications such as liver damage and endocrine dysfunction. The condition may also lead to dysfunction of the parathyroid glands, potentially causing hypoparathyroidism and hypocalcemia. Hemochromatosis can be caused by genetic factors and is often associated with enzymatic deficiencies that result in pathological iron deposits. To manage the condition and prevent complications, dietary control of iron intake is essential. Additionally, it has been noted that hemochromatosis may be related to elevated copper levels in some cases.\",31\r\n12973,CIRROSIS BILIAR FOCAL,\"Focal biliary cirrhosis is a liver condition that can develop in patients with cystic fibrosis, characterized by damage to the bile ducts.\",2\r\n2213,CIRROSIS HEPÁTICA,\"Cirrhosis hepática, or hepatic cirrhosis, is a late-stage liver disease characterized by extensive scarring (fibrosis) of the liver, which is often irreversible. This chronic condition can arise from various liver diseases and conditions, including hepatitis, chronic alcoholism, and glucogenosis due to prolonged liver damage. Cirrhosis can lead to significant complications, such as ascitis quilosa, a condition involving fluid accumulation in the abdominal cavity, and digestive hemorrhage, particularly complicating medical procedures like the placement of gastrostomy tubes. \n\nAdditionally, cirrhosis can affect the body's ability to clear prolactin, which may have further implications for health. Management of cirrhosis often requires dietary modifications, such as salt restriction, to help induce a negative sodium balance and manage symptoms. Overall, cirrhosis hepática represents a serious health concern that necessitates careful monitoring and treatment to mitigate its complications.\",26\r\n11706,HH,\"HH, or hereditary hemochromatosis, is a genetic condition that causes excessive iron absorption and accumulation in the body, leading to various health issues.\",12\r\n4512,FERRITINA,\"Ferritina, also known as ferritin, is a crucial protein that stores iron in the body, playing a vital role in maintaining iron homeostasis and preventing anemia. It functions by releasing iron when needed, thus regulating iron levels within cells. Ferritin levels are commonly measured to evaluate the body's iron stores, which is particularly important for patients with eating disorders and those suffering from conditions such as hemochromatosis. Elevated ferritin levels can occur in various medical conditions, including hyperthyroidism and inflammatory bowel disease, even in cases of iron deficiency. Additionally, ferritin serves as a significant biomarker for assessing iron levels in the blood, making it an essential component in diagnosing and managing various health conditions related to iron metabolism.\",7\r\n9498,BIOPSIA HEPÁTICA,\"BIOPSIA HEPÁTICA, or liver biopsy, is a medical procedure that involves the extraction of a small sample of liver tissue for analysis. This diagnostic procedure is utilized to confirm various liver conditions, including Glycogen Storage Disease Type 0 (GSD0), by studying the liver tissue. Additionally, it plays a crucial role in assessing liver damage due to iron overload in hereditary hemochromatosis and can reveal copper deposits in the liver, which is important for confirming Wilson's disease. Overall, the biopsia hepática is an essential tool in the diagnosis and management of various liver-related health issues.\",4\r\n4868,HIPERTENSIÓN PORTAL,\"HIPERTENSIÓN PORTAL refers to a medical condition characterized by increased blood pressure in the portal venous system, which is often a complication associated with chronic liver diseases, particularly cirrhosis. This condition contributes to the retention of fluids in patients suffering from liver cirrhosis and can complicate medical procedures such as the placement of gastrostomy tubes due to the heightened risk of bleeding. Overall, portal hypertension is a significant concern in the management of liver-related health issues, especially in the context of cirrhosis.\",4\r\n11699,FLEBOTOMÍAS,\"FLEBOTOMÍAS refers to medical procedures that involve the extraction of blood from a patient. These procedures are particularly significant in the treatment of conditions such as hemochromatosis, where they are employed to reduce excess iron levels in the body. By performing phlebotomy, healthcare providers can help alleviate iron overload, which is crucial for preventing serious complications, including cirrhosis and diabetes. Overall, phlebotomy serves as a therapeutic intervention aimed at managing iron levels and improving patient health.\",3\r\n11649,MUTACIÓN C282Y,\"MUTACIÓN C282Y refers to a specific mutation in the HFE gene that is commonly associated with hereditary hemochromatosis. This mutation is the most prevalent cause of type 1 hemochromatosis, which significantly impacts the body's ability to absorb iron. As a genetic alteration, C282Y leads to hereditary hemochromatosis, resulting in an excessive accumulation of iron in the body.\",3\r\n11661,C282Y,\"C282Y is a specific mutation in the HFE gene that is associated with hereditary hemochromatosis, a condition that can lead to iron overload in the body.\",2\r\n11648,GEN HFE,\"The HFE gene, located on the short arm of chromosome 6, is associated with iron metabolism and plays a crucial role in the regulation of iron levels in the body. Mutations in the HFE gene are linked to hereditary hemochromatosis, a genetic disorder characterized by excessive iron accumulation in the body, which can lead to various health complications.\",2\r\n11650,MUTACIÓN H63D,\"La mutación H63D en el gen HFE es una alteración genética que puede asociarse con la sobrecarga férrica, especialmente cuando se presenta en combinación con la mutación C282Y. Aunque la mutación H63D está relacionada con la hemocromatosis, su efecto es considerado menos severo en comparación con el de la mutación C282Y.\",2\r\n11658,MACRÓFAGOS,\"MACRÓFAGOS, or macrophages, are immune cells that play a crucial role in the body's defense mechanisms. They are particularly stimulated in the context of inflammatory bowel diseases, where they contribute to the immune response. Additionally, macrophages are involved in iron metabolism, functioning by storing and releasing iron as needed. This dual role highlights their importance not only in immune responses but also in maintaining iron homeostasis within the body.\",2\r\n11659,ARTICULACIONES,\"Joints can also accumulate excess iron, leading to complications in hemochromatosis.\",1\r\n11655,DMT1,DMT1 is a transporter that facilitates the absorption of ferrous iron across the intestinal membrane.,1\r\n11656,FERROPORTINA,Ferroportin is a transporter that exports iron from cells into the bloodstream.,1\r\n11654,HEPCIDINA,\"Hepcidin is a small peptide that regulates iron absorption and release in the body, playing a crucial role in iron homeostasis.\",1\r\n11660,S65C,S65C is another mutation related to iron overload when associated with the C282Y mutation in hereditary hemochromatosis.,1\r\n2214,CARCINOMA HEPATOCELULAR,\"Carcinoma hepatocellular, also known as hepatocellular carcinoma, is a primary malignancy of the liver that is frequently linked to chronic liver disease and cirrhosis. This type of liver cancer can also arise in patients with hereditary hemochromatosis, especially in the presence of cirrhosis.\",2\r\n76,RESONANCIA MAGNÉTICA,\"Resonancia Mag